                <pb facs="0262" n="254" sameAs="frus:frus1938v01/pg254"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.00/8603: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bowers</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic">Then in France, to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">St.
                                Jean de Luz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-11-12T13:00:00+00:00">November 12, 1938—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 12—10:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>556. Hemming went to London but returns to Burgos. I learn he does not pretend to
                    represent the Non-intervention Committee but does represent the four Munich
                    powers with Portugal thrown in for good measure. He says he will remain in
                    Burgos until Franco signs some carefully guarded plan submitted to him but no
                    indications of nature of plan. Generally assumed here it must be in “the spirit
                    of Munich”. Hemming says doubtfully he hopes also to go to Barcelona but as
                    representative of the Munich spirit he will not be welcome there.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bowers</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
